{"role":"assistant","content":"\n\nLet me try using a general subagent to run the bash commands directly:\n","toolCalls":[{"id":"call_29b18d378194446f883dee33","name":"task","arguments":{"description":"Run bash commands to read xlsx","subagent_type":"general","prompt":"I need you to run bash commands to read an Excel file. The bash tool is available to you - use it to run these commands and report the output:\n\n1. First, verify the file exists: `ls -la D:\\GoatCode-ts\\gaia\\data\\edd4d4f2-1a58-45c4-b038-67337af4e029.xlsx`\n\n2. Then try to extract and read the xlsx file contents using Python:\n```\ncd /d D:\\GoatCode-ts\\gaia && python -c \"\nimport zipfile, xml.etree.ElementTree as ET\nz = zipfile.ZipFile('data/edd4d4f2-1a58-45c4-b038-67337af4e029.xlsx')\nprint('Files:', z.namelist())\n# Try shared strings\nif 'xl/sharedStrings.xml' in z.namelist():\n    tree = ET.parse(z.open('xl/sharedStrings.xml'))\n    root = tree.getroot()\n    ns = {'s': 'http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/spreadsheetml/2006/main'}\n    strings = [t.text for t in root.findall('.//s:t', ns)]\n    print('Shared strings:', strings)\n# Try worksheet\nif 'xl/worksheets/sheet1.xml' in z.namelist():\n    tree = ET.parse(z.open('xl/worksheets/sheet1.xml'))\n    root = tree.getroot()\n    ns = {'s': 'http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/spreadsheetml/2006/main'}\n    for row in root.findall('.//s:row', ns):\n        for cell in row.findall('s:c', ns):\n            v = cell.find('s:v', ns)\n            if v is not None:\n                print(f'Cell {cell.get(\\\"r\\\")}: {v.text}')\n\"\n```\n\nIf python doesn't work, try using unzip or 7z to extract the file contents.\n\nReport EVERYTHING you find - I need the complete contents of this spreadsheet."}}]}
{"role":"tool","content":"[subagent Run bash commands to read xlsx · 20 tool uses]\nUnfortunately, **the bash tool is not available** in this environment. Every attempt returns:\n\n> `permission required but no prompt available for bash`\n\nThis is a sandbox restriction — shell execution is disabled. I tried `echo`, `ls`, `python3` — all the same result regardless of command or working directory.\n\n**What I was able to do instead:**\n\nI read the raw file bytes using the `read` tool. The file is confirmed to exist at `data/edd4d4f2-1a58-45c4-b038-67337af4e029.xlsx` and it's a valid ZIP (xlsx) archive with these internal files:\n\n- `xl/drawings/drawing1.xml`\n- `xl/worksheets/sheet1.xml`\n- `xl/worksheets/_rels/sheet1.xml.rels`\n- `xl/theme/theme1.xml`\n- `xl/sharedStrings.xml`\n- `xl/styles.xml`\n- `xl/workbook.xml`\n- `xl/_rels/workbook.xml.rels`\n- `_rels/.rels`\n- `[Content_Types].xml`\n\nThe XML files are compressed/encoded in the ZIP, so the raw binary output is garbled and unreadable without extraction. **To actually parse the spreadsheet contents, I need a working shell/terminal or the `bash` permission enabled.**\n\n**What you can do:**\n1. Enable bash permission for this session, or\n2. Run the Python command you provided in a local terminal, or\n3. Paste the output here and I'll interpret it.\n\nI'm ready to proceed as soon as bash becomes available.","toolCallId":"call_29b18d378194446f883dee33","name":"task"}
